1
00:00:00,330 --> 00:00:01,439
Just after midnight,

2
00:00:01,489 --> 00:00:03,989
ten men walked out of a jail in New Orleans.

3
00:00:04,039 --> 00:00:06,365
Nobody noticed for more than seven hours.

4
00:00:07,715 --> 00:00:10,121
They pulled a cell door off its track.

5
00:00:10,171 --> 00:00:12,189
Ripped a toilet out of the wall.

6
00:00:12,239 --> 00:00:15,808
Crawled through the hole behind it. And above that hole,

7
00:00:15,858 --> 00:00:18,135
they left a message. To easy. L O L.

8
00:00:18,885 --> 00:00:22,316
How did ten men get out before anyone realized they were gone?

9
00:00:22,366 --> 00:00:25,572
Who turned off the water? And why did it take five months,

10
00:00:25,622 --> 00:00:28,323
and a standoff in Atlanta, to catch the last one?

11
00:00:29,293 --> 00:00:30,847
Chapter 1. The jail.

12
00:00:32,977 --> 00:00:36,104
The Orleans Parish jail sits in Mid City New Orleans,

13
00:00:36,154 --> 00:00:40,421
right next to Interstate 10. The main building opened in twenty fifteen.

14
00:00:40,471 --> 00:00:43,418
It cost one hundred and forty six million dollars,

15
00:00:43,468 --> 00:00:45,817
and has more than fourteen hundred beds.

16
00:00:46,567 --> 00:00:49,674
It replaced one of the most notorious jails in America.

17
00:00:49,724 --> 00:00:54,899
The old Orleans Parish Prison became infamous after Hurricane Katrina in two thousand five,

18
00:00:54,949 --> 00:00:58,917
when hundreds of prisoners were left in their cells as the water rose.

19
00:00:59,667 --> 00:01:01,435
According to Human Rights Watch,

20
00:01:01,485 --> 00:01:06,889
around six hundred and fifty prisoners were left in one of the buildings as the floodwater rose,

21
00:01:06,939 --> 00:01:10,468
and hundreds of prisoners were later listed as unaccounted for.

22
00:01:10,518 --> 00:01:14,445
Some old buildings were abandoned after the storm, and never reopened.

23
00:01:15,195 --> 00:01:16,334
For years afterwards,

24
00:01:16,384 --> 00:01:21,146
the jail was under federal court supervision because of violence and poor conditions.

25
00:01:21,196 --> 00:01:23,807
New Orleans needed a jail it could be proud of.

26
00:01:24,557 --> 00:01:28,504
The new building was supposed to be a fresh start. Modern design.

27
00:01:28,554 --> 00:01:32,870
Cameras everywhere. Sliding cell doors controlled from secure stations.

28
00:01:33,620 --> 00:01:36,904
But by twenty twenty five, the jail had serious problems.

29
00:01:36,954 --> 00:01:41,056
Federal monitors had already warned that conditions were getting worse,

30
00:01:41,106 --> 00:01:43,454
and blamed a lack of resources and staff.

31
00:01:43,504 --> 00:01:46,846
Many of the cell door locks were faulty. After the escape,

32
00:01:46,896 --> 00:01:52,870
the Louisiana attorney general estimated that around one hundred and sixty locks needed to be replaced.

33
00:01:53,620 --> 00:01:55,010
The sheriff, Susan Hutson,

34
00:01:55,060 --> 00:02:00,605
said her office had been asking local officials for millions of dollars to fix those locks for years.

35
00:02:00,655 --> 00:02:01,768
The money never came.

36
00:02:02,518 --> 00:02:06,142
Most of the people inside had not been convicted of anything.

37
00:02:06,192 --> 00:02:07,828
They were waiting for trial.

38
00:02:07,878 --> 00:02:12,406
But some of them were facing very serious charges. Murder. Attempted murder.

39
00:02:12,456 --> 00:02:13,249
Armed robbery.

40
00:02:13,999 --> 00:02:17,015
On the night of May fifteenth, twenty twenty five,

41
00:02:17,065 --> 00:02:20,755
the jail was placed on lockdown at ten thirty in the evening.

42
00:02:20,805 --> 00:02:22,717
A normal night. Or so it seemed.

43
00:02:23,687 --> 00:02:25,937
Chapter 2. Twelve twenty three.

44
00:02:28,067 --> 00:02:31,839
At twelve twenty three in the morning, on Friday, May sixteenth,

45
00:02:31,889 --> 00:02:35,959
a security camera inside one housing unit recorded something strange.

46
00:02:36,709 --> 00:02:38,947
Several inmates were out of their cells.

47
00:02:38,997 --> 00:02:42,037
They were working on the sliding door of another cell.

48
00:02:42,087 --> 00:02:46,614
Pulling at it. Pushing it. For about twenty minutes, according to investigators.

49
00:02:47,364 --> 00:02:48,835
Then the door came off its track.

50
00:02:50,585 --> 00:02:52,761
The cell they opened was a special one.

51
00:02:52,811 --> 00:02:56,472
A cell for disabled inmates. According to one lawyer in the case,

52
00:02:56,522 --> 00:02:58,641
it should not have been in use at all.

53
00:02:59,391 --> 00:03:02,352
Inside that cell, the men went to work on the wall.

54
00:03:02,402 --> 00:03:05,657
They ripped out the combined steel sink and toilet unit.

55
00:03:05,707 --> 00:03:08,550
Behind it was a gap in the wall, with metal bars.

56
00:03:08,600 --> 00:03:10,085
They cut through the bars.

57
00:03:10,835 --> 00:03:14,664
Normally, ripping out a toilet would flood the cell. But that night,

58
00:03:14,714 --> 00:03:18,031
the water to the toilet had been turned off. Remember that.

59
00:03:18,081 --> 00:03:19,229
It will matter later.

60
00:03:19,979 --> 00:03:23,501
Before they left, someone wrote a message on the wall,

61
00:03:23,551 --> 00:03:26,875
above the hole. To easy, L O L. Spelled with one o.

62
00:03:26,925 --> 00:03:30,315
With an arrow pointing down at the hole. Next to it,

63
00:03:30,365 --> 00:03:32,234
another message. We innocent.

64
00:03:33,784 --> 00:03:36,798
And where was the staff? According to the authorities,

65
00:03:36,848 --> 00:03:40,657
the person assigned to watch that area had stepped away to get food.

66
00:03:40,707 --> 00:03:42,416
And according to later reports,

67
00:03:42,466 --> 00:03:46,559
a jail employee watching the surveillance cameras saw part of the escape,

68
00:03:46,609 --> 00:03:47,808
and did not report it.

69
00:03:48,558 --> 00:03:49,525
Think about that.

70
00:03:49,575 --> 00:03:52,337
A cell door that could be pulled off its track.

71
00:03:52,387 --> 00:03:55,089
A toilet that could be pulled out of the wall.

72
00:03:55,139 --> 00:03:57,722
Bars that could be cut. And nobody watching.

73
00:03:57,772 --> 00:04:01,312
Every barrier in that cell had a weak point. And that night,

74
00:04:01,362 --> 00:04:02,808
every one of them failed.

75
00:04:03,778 --> 00:04:05,772
Chapter 3. Over the wall.

76
00:04:07,902 --> 00:04:11,475
One by one, ten men squeezed through the hole behind the toilet.

77
00:04:12,225 --> 00:04:13,740
At around one in the morning,

78
00:04:13,790 --> 00:04:17,196
a second camera caught them in a loading dock area, moving fast,

79
00:04:17,246 --> 00:04:20,058
toward a part of the jail grounds under construction.

80
00:04:20,808 --> 00:04:24,496
Then came the last barrier. A fence, topped with barbed wire.

81
00:04:24,546 --> 00:04:27,866
The men threw blankets over the wire, and climbed over.

82
00:04:28,616 --> 00:04:30,525
On the other side was Interstate 10.

83
00:04:30,575 --> 00:04:34,388
A traffic camera recorded them running across the highway, in the dark,

84
00:04:34,438 --> 00:04:36,892
and disappearing into the neighborhood beyond.

85
00:04:37,642 --> 00:04:41,279
Investigators later found discarded clothing on the other side,

86
00:04:41,329 --> 00:04:43,327
believed to belong to the escapees.

87
00:04:44,077 --> 00:04:45,863
From the first tampering with the door,

88
00:04:45,913 --> 00:04:47,322
to the last man over the fence,

89
00:04:47,372 --> 00:04:49,159
the whole thing took less than an hour.

90
00:04:49,909 --> 00:04:52,506
And inside the jail, nobody raised the alarm.

91
00:04:54,476 --> 00:04:56,226
Chapter 4. The headcount.

92
00:04:58,356 --> 00:05:02,203
The night went on. Shifts changed. The cameras kept recording.

93
00:05:02,253 --> 00:05:03,837
And ten beds stayed empty.

94
00:05:04,587 --> 00:05:08,213
At eight thirty in the morning, staff carried out a routine headcount.

95
00:05:08,263 --> 00:05:10,786
The numbers did not add up. Ten men were missing.

96
00:05:12,136 --> 00:05:15,325
Jail leadership was told at eight thirty five. By then,

97
00:05:15,375 --> 00:05:18,447
the escapees had been gone for more than seven hours.

98
00:05:18,497 --> 00:05:23,513
One account later put the delay in reporting to other agencies at more than ten hours.

99
00:05:24,263 --> 00:05:26,171
Seven hours is a long head start.

100
00:05:26,221 --> 00:05:28,249
Enough time to get across the city.

101
00:05:28,299 --> 00:05:30,682
Enough time to get across the state line.

102
00:05:31,432 --> 00:05:34,588
Now the sheriff's office had to tell the public that ten men,

103
00:05:34,638 --> 00:05:37,741
several of them accused of murder, were somewhere out there.

104
00:05:37,791 --> 00:05:39,580
And that nobody knew exactly where.

105
00:05:40,330 --> 00:05:43,684
Sheriff Hutson made a statement that would follow her for months.

106
00:05:43,734 --> 00:05:45,412
It's almost impossible, she said,

107
00:05:45,462 --> 00:05:48,188
for anybody to get out of this facility without help.

108
00:05:48,938 --> 00:05:49,764
In other words,

109
00:05:49,814 --> 00:05:53,677
the sheriff was saying that someone inside her own jail had helped.

110
00:05:53,727 --> 00:05:56,889
Within days, she would be proven at least partly right.

111
00:05:57,859 --> 00:05:59,447
Chapter 5. The ten.

112
00:06:01,577 --> 00:06:02,885
So who were the ten men?

113
00:06:03,635 --> 00:06:05,180
According to the authorities,

114
00:06:05,230 --> 00:06:08,313
six of them were charged with murder or attempted murder.

115
00:06:08,363 --> 00:06:10,622
Almost all of them were waiting for trial.

116
00:06:11,372 --> 00:06:15,144
The most dangerous, in the eyes of police, was Derrick Groves.

117
00:06:15,194 --> 00:06:18,719
Twenty seven years old. Convicted of second degree murder,

118
00:06:18,769 --> 00:06:20,938
and facing a possible life sentence.

119
00:06:21,688 --> 00:06:24,183
Then there was Antoine Massey, thirty two,

120
00:06:24,233 --> 00:06:28,849
held on charges including domestic abuse and car theft. According to reports,

121
00:06:28,899 --> 00:06:32,666
this was his third escape from a jail since two thousand seven.

122
00:06:32,716 --> 00:06:35,817
He had previously cut off electronic ankle monitors.

123
00:06:36,567 --> 00:06:40,619
Lenton Vanburen, accused of a murder in New Orleans East.

124
00:06:40,669 --> 00:06:46,376
Jermaine Donald and Leo Tate, both facing serious charges. Corey Boyd, nineteen.

125
00:06:46,426 --> 00:06:51,918
Gary Price, twenty one. Dkenan Dennis, twenty four. Robert Moody, twenty one.

126
00:06:51,968 --> 00:06:53,789
And Kendell Myles, twenty.

127
00:06:54,539 --> 00:06:55,989
Ten men. Ten mugshots.

128
00:06:56,039 --> 00:06:58,511
And a city told to call nine one one,

129
00:06:58,561 --> 00:07:00,215
and not to approach them.

130
00:07:00,965 --> 00:07:02,268
Most of them were young.

131
00:07:02,318 --> 00:07:04,749
Five of the ten were twenty four or younger.

132
00:07:04,799 --> 00:07:08,076
And most of them were from New Orleans. They knew the city.

133
00:07:08,126 --> 00:07:09,373
And the city knew them.

134
00:07:10,343 --> 00:07:12,024
Chapter 6. The hunt.

135
00:07:14,154 --> 00:07:17,310
The manhunt started fast. More than two hundred local,

136
00:07:17,360 --> 00:07:21,525
state and federal officers joined the search. The FBI, the US Marshals,

137
00:07:21,575 --> 00:07:23,662
the ATF, and Louisiana State Police.

138
00:07:24,412 --> 00:07:26,674
The first capture came the same morning.

139
00:07:26,724 --> 00:07:30,548
Kendell Myles was spotted on Bourbon Street, in the French Quarter,

140
00:07:30,598 --> 00:07:34,074
less than two miles from the jail. State troopers chased him.

141
00:07:34,124 --> 00:07:37,427
He was found hiding under a car in a hotel parking garage.

142
00:07:38,177 --> 00:07:40,181
By the evening of that first day,

143
00:07:40,231 --> 00:07:42,920
two more were back in custody. Robert Moody,

144
00:07:42,970 --> 00:07:45,907
after a tip to Crimestoppers. And Dkenan Dennis,

145
00:07:45,957 --> 00:07:47,650
in another part of the city.

146
00:07:48,400 --> 00:07:52,051
Three escapees caught in a single day. According to reports,

147
00:07:52,101 --> 00:07:56,739
they were flown by helicopter to the Louisiana State Penitentiary at Angola.

148
00:07:56,789 --> 00:07:58,651
But seven were still out there.

149
00:07:59,901 --> 00:08:04,347
On Monday, May nineteenth, Gary Price was arrested in New Orleans East.

150
00:08:04,397 --> 00:08:07,259
On Tuesday, Corey Boyd was caught in the city.

151
00:08:08,009 --> 00:08:10,455
And then the hunt moved beyond New Orleans.

152
00:08:11,205 --> 00:08:15,026
On May twenty sixth, police in Baton Rouge got an anonymous tip.

153
00:08:15,076 --> 00:08:17,022
Shortly after six in the evening,

154
00:08:17,072 --> 00:08:20,530
officers stopped a man sitting on a bench outside a store.

155
00:08:20,580 --> 00:08:21,921
It was Lenton Vanburen.

156
00:08:22,671 --> 00:08:26,637
The same day, hundreds of kilometers away in Walker County, Texas,

157
00:08:26,687 --> 00:08:30,289
north of Houston, state troopers chased a car at high speed.

158
00:08:30,339 --> 00:08:34,001
Inside were Jermaine Donald and Leo Tate. Both were arrested.

159
00:08:34,751 --> 00:08:37,643
Eight of ten were back in custody in just ten days.

160
00:08:37,693 --> 00:08:41,912
The reward for each of the last two was raised to twenty thousand dollars.

161
00:08:42,662 --> 00:08:47,984
Louisiana's governor, Jeff Landry, posted a message online, celebrating the arrests.

162
00:08:48,034 --> 00:08:52,141
And then he addressed the last two directly. Antoine and Derrick.

163
00:08:52,191 --> 00:08:52,972
You are next.

164
00:08:53,722 --> 00:08:56,333
For investigators, the pattern was becoming clear.

165
00:08:56,383 --> 00:08:59,313
The escapees were not hiding in the swamps or the woods.

166
00:08:59,363 --> 00:09:01,442
They were going to the people they knew.

167
00:09:01,492 --> 00:09:04,102
Which meant that every arrest led to the next one.

168
00:09:05,072 --> 00:09:07,543
Chapter 7. Friends and family.

169
00:09:09,673 --> 00:09:12,374
The escapees had not done it alone. At least,

170
00:09:12,424 --> 00:09:14,025
not after they got outside.

171
00:09:14,775 --> 00:09:16,634
In the weeks after the escape,

172
00:09:16,684 --> 00:09:21,854
the police arrested people accused of helping them. Girlfriends. Cousins. Friends.

173
00:09:21,904 --> 00:09:24,273
And in some cases, their own families.

174
00:09:25,023 --> 00:09:27,884
A fifty nine year old grandmother was arrested,

175
00:09:27,934 --> 00:09:31,476
accused of helping her grandson. Lenton Vanburen's father,

176
00:09:31,526 --> 00:09:34,325
sister and another relative were arrested too.

177
00:09:34,375 --> 00:09:37,422
Some of them had bonds set at one million dollars.

178
00:09:38,172 --> 00:09:41,873
One man was accused of paying for a hotel room in Baton Rouge.

179
00:09:41,923 --> 00:09:45,744
Another woman was accused of driving two of the escapees around.

180
00:09:45,794 --> 00:09:48,829
And Derrick Groves's girlfriend was arrested later.

181
00:09:49,579 --> 00:09:52,609
Even inside the jail, the net widened. Another inmate,

182
00:09:52,659 --> 00:09:56,373
who had not escaped, was charged with ten counts of simple escape,

183
00:09:56,423 --> 00:09:58,540
accused of helping the others get out.

184
00:09:59,290 --> 00:09:59,829
In total,

185
00:09:59,879 --> 00:10:04,147
sixteen people were accused of helping the escape or the escapees.

186
00:10:04,197 --> 00:10:08,857
Fourteen friends and relatives. One other inmate. And one jail employee.

187
00:10:09,607 --> 00:10:12,309
That jail employee was the man who turned off the water.

188
00:10:14,079 --> 00:10:15,725
Chapter 8. The water.

189
00:10:17,855 --> 00:10:19,710
His name was Sterling Williams.

190
00:10:19,760 --> 00:10:22,168
He was a maintenance worker at the jail,

191
00:10:22,218 --> 00:10:25,794
sometimes described as a plumber, sometimes as a custodian.

192
00:10:26,544 --> 00:10:31,376
According to the investigators' affidavit, one of the escapees, Antoine Massey,

193
00:10:31,426 --> 00:10:34,218
told Williams to shut off the water to a cell.

194
00:10:34,268 --> 00:10:38,976
Williams told investigators that Massey threatened to stab him if he did not.

195
00:10:39,726 --> 00:10:42,162
Williams admitted that he turned off the water.

196
00:10:42,212 --> 00:10:43,802
And without water in the pipes,

197
00:10:43,852 --> 00:10:47,135
the inmates could rip out the toilet without flooding the cell,

198
00:10:47,185 --> 00:10:48,722
and without drawing attention.

199
00:10:49,472 --> 00:10:52,294
With the water turned off, the investigator wrote,

200
00:10:52,344 --> 00:10:56,028
the inmates were able to successfully make good on their escapes.

201
00:10:57,378 --> 00:10:59,240
Williams was arrested days later,

202
00:10:59,290 --> 00:11:02,253
and charged with ten counts connected to the escape,

203
00:11:02,303 --> 00:11:03,760
and malfeasance in office.

204
00:11:04,510 --> 00:11:07,089
His lawyer told a very different story.

205
00:11:07,139 --> 00:11:09,787
He said the toilet had been overflowing,

206
00:11:09,837 --> 00:11:12,214
after inmates clogged it on purpose.

207
00:11:12,264 --> 00:11:15,249
A female deputy asked Williams to look at it.

208
00:11:15,299 --> 00:11:18,081
No work order was made. Williams, he said,

209
00:11:18,131 --> 00:11:21,453
did not conspire with anyone. He was used. A tool,

210
00:11:21,503 --> 00:11:23,543
his lawyer said, not a partner.

211
00:11:24,293 --> 00:11:28,713
It was not the first time an American jailbreak had depended on someone on the inside.

212
00:11:29,683 --> 00:11:31,306
Chapter 9. Not the first.

213
00:11:33,436 --> 00:11:37,627
In June twenty fifteen, two convicted murderers, Richard Matt and David Sweat,

214
00:11:37,677 --> 00:11:41,432
escaped from the Clinton Correctional Facility in Dannemora, New York.

215
00:11:41,482 --> 00:11:44,042
They cut through the steel walls of their cells,

216
00:11:44,092 --> 00:11:47,032
crawled through the pipes and tunnels under the prison,

217
00:11:47,082 --> 00:11:49,641
and came up through a manhole outside the walls.

218
00:11:50,391 --> 00:11:53,371
They left a note behind too. A yellow sticky note,

219
00:11:53,421 --> 00:11:56,461
with a smiley face, and the words, have a nice day.

220
00:11:57,211 --> 00:12:01,133
The tools had been smuggled in by a prison worker, Joyce Mitchell,

221
00:12:01,183 --> 00:12:05,045
who later went to prison herself. The manhunt lasted three weeks.

222
00:12:05,095 --> 00:12:08,114
Richard Matt was shot and killed by federal agents.

223
00:12:08,164 --> 00:12:11,062
David Sweat was shot and captured two days later.

224
00:12:11,812 --> 00:12:14,733
The New Orleans escape was cruder. No tunnels.

225
00:12:14,783 --> 00:12:18,608
No hacksaw blades hidden in frozen meat. Just a broken door,

226
00:12:18,658 --> 00:12:21,966
a toilet, and a fence. But the pattern was the same.

227
00:12:22,016 --> 00:12:25,130
A system that relied on everyone doing their job.

228
00:12:25,180 --> 00:12:27,003
And a few people who did not.

229
00:12:27,973 --> 00:12:30,026
Chapter 10. Five months.

230
00:12:32,156 --> 00:12:35,877
By June, only two men were still free. Antoine Massey,

231
00:12:35,927 --> 00:12:37,204
and Derrick Groves.

232
00:12:37,954 --> 00:12:42,626
Massey did something few fugitives would do. He posted videos on Instagram,

233
00:12:42,676 --> 00:12:44,264
insisting he was innocent.

234
00:12:44,314 --> 00:12:47,097
Police raided a house where he had been seen.

235
00:12:47,147 --> 00:12:48,357
He was already gone.

236
00:12:49,107 --> 00:12:53,984
On June twenty seventh, Antoine Massey was finally captured in New Orleans.

237
00:12:54,034 --> 00:12:55,364
Nine down. One to go.

238
00:12:56,714 --> 00:13:01,241
And Derrick Groves simply vanished. Weeks became months. Summer ended.

239
00:13:01,291 --> 00:13:06,211
Investigators kept following tips, phone records, and the people around him.

240
00:13:06,961 --> 00:13:10,529
In June, his girlfriend was arrested, accused of helping him.

241
00:13:10,579 --> 00:13:13,080
But Groves himself was nowhere to be found.

242
00:13:13,130 --> 00:13:15,749
Investigators believed he had left Louisiana.

243
00:13:16,499 --> 00:13:20,567
Then, in October, a tip came in to Crimestoppers in New Orleans.

244
00:13:20,617 --> 00:13:25,135
It pointed to a house in Atlanta, Georgia, hundreds of kilometers away.

245
00:13:25,885 --> 00:13:28,074
On October eighth, twenty twenty five,

246
00:13:28,124 --> 00:13:32,081
police surrounded the house in south west Atlanta. After a standoff,

247
00:13:32,131 --> 00:13:33,613
according to news reports,

248
00:13:33,663 --> 00:13:36,854
Groves was found hiding in a crawl space underneath it.

249
00:13:37,604 --> 00:13:40,283
He had been on the run for almost five months.

250
00:13:40,333 --> 00:13:43,546
He was the tenth and last of the escapees to be caught.

251
00:13:45,316 --> 00:13:47,334
Chapter 11. The fallout.

252
00:13:49,464 --> 00:13:53,014
The escape did not just embarrass the jail. It ended careers.

253
00:13:53,764 --> 00:13:58,292
Days after the escape, three jail employees were suspended without pay.

254
00:13:58,342 --> 00:13:59,323
Then three more.

255
00:14:00,073 --> 00:14:03,510
Sheriff Susan Hutson was running for re election that year.

256
00:14:03,560 --> 00:14:07,056
In October twenty twenty five, she finished a distant third,

257
00:14:07,106 --> 00:14:10,897
with around seventeen percent of the vote. Michelle Woodfork won,

258
00:14:10,947 --> 00:14:13,970
and became the new sheriff in May twenty twenty six.

259
00:14:14,720 --> 00:14:16,980
Louisiana's attorney general, Liz Murrill,

260
00:14:17,030 --> 00:14:21,601
said a state investigation found that Hutson's poor management led to the jailbreak.

261
00:14:22,351 --> 00:14:24,216
And in April twenty twenty six,

262
00:14:24,266 --> 00:14:28,356
a special grand jury indicted Susan Hutson on thirty felony counts,

263
00:14:28,406 --> 00:14:33,052
including malfeasance, obstruction of justice and falsifying public records.

264
00:14:33,102 --> 00:14:37,130
Her chief financial officer was indicted on twenty similar counts.

265
00:14:37,180 --> 00:14:38,922
Both have pleaded not guilty.

266
00:14:38,972 --> 00:14:42,135
Hutson is not accused of helping the inmates escape.

267
00:14:42,885 --> 00:14:45,714
Then, on October fifteenth, twenty twenty six,

268
00:14:45,764 --> 00:14:48,907
Sterling Williams stood in a New Orleans courtroom.

269
00:14:49,657 --> 00:14:52,964
He pleaded guilty to one count of malfeasance in office.

270
00:14:53,014 --> 00:14:55,542
Prosecutors dropped the ten escape charges.

271
00:14:55,592 --> 00:14:58,420
The judge sentenced him to five years in prison,

272
00:14:58,470 --> 00:15:01,777
with half of it suspended, and three years of probation.

273
00:15:02,527 --> 00:15:06,034
His lawyer said Williams accepted that he had made mistakes.

274
00:15:06,084 --> 00:15:07,753
He had not followed protocol.

275
00:15:07,803 --> 00:15:10,421
But he had never meant to help anyone escape.

276
00:15:11,171 --> 00:15:12,575
As for the ten escapees,

277
00:15:12,625 --> 00:15:15,423
they now face new charges of aggravated escape,

278
00:15:15,473 --> 00:15:18,453
on top of everything they were already accused of.

279
00:15:18,503 --> 00:15:20,271
Their trial was set for April.

280
00:15:21,021 --> 00:15:21,999
And the jail itself?

281
00:15:22,049 --> 00:15:25,854
The faulty locks became a question of who should have paid to replace them,

282
00:15:25,904 --> 00:15:28,218
and why it had not happened sooner. Meanwhile,

283
00:15:28,268 --> 00:15:33,152
the building that was supposed to be a fresh start had become famous for a hole behind a toilet.

284
00:15:34,122 --> 00:15:36,129
Chapter 12. Too easy.

285
00:15:38,259 --> 00:15:41,209
So how did it happen? Not through one big failure,

286
00:15:41,259 --> 00:15:43,249
but through a chain of small ones.

287
00:15:43,999 --> 00:15:47,551
A broken lock. A cell that should not have been in use.

288
00:15:47,601 --> 00:15:50,040
Water turned off without a work order.

289
00:15:50,090 --> 00:15:51,808
A guard away from the post.

290
00:15:51,858 --> 00:15:54,493
A camera operator who did not call it in.

291
00:15:54,543 --> 00:15:56,065
A headcount hours later.

292
00:15:56,815 --> 00:15:58,906
Any one of those links, if it had held,

293
00:15:58,956 --> 00:16:01,213
might have stopped them. None of them did.

294
00:16:02,763 --> 00:16:07,294
And the message on the wall said exactly what the prisoners thought about it.

295
00:16:07,344 --> 00:16:08,187
To easy. L O L.

296
00:16:09,937 --> 00:16:13,430
So what do you think? Who carries the most blame? The sheriff,

297
00:16:13,480 --> 00:16:16,174
the system, or the man who turned off the water?

298
00:16:16,224 --> 00:16:17,546
Tell us in the comments.

299
00:16:17,596 --> 00:16:21,375
And if you want to see an escape planned like a military operation,

300
00:16:21,425 --> 00:16:25,776
watch our story about the man who escaped from a French prison by helicopter,

301
00:16:25,826 --> 00:16:27,377
in seven and a half minutes.
